The agent's writing-style skill now carries a catalog of the patterns readers use to spot AI prose, with an instruction to never produce them: the rule of three, "not X, but Y" parallelisms, em dashes, the delve/robust/seamless lexicon, "serves as" where the sentence means "is", synonym cycling, significance inflation ("plays a vital role"), hedging filler ("it's worth noting"), assistant chirp, bolded-phrase bullets, and closing summaries. The catalog is drawn from Wikipedia's Signs of AI writing field guide and the vocabulary studies it cites (Juzek and Ward on RLHF word overuse, Kobak on academic abstracts).

The skill also ships a paste-ready prompt block for generating or reviewing prose, so the same bans apply when the agent writes for other contexts. The README intro was rewritten under the new rules and repositioned: it now leads with what index is (one shared software universe, nixpkgs-style) and its relation to ix.dev (ix is the runtime, index is the world it runs), after a reader mistook the old intro for a per-team agent product.
